

The concurrency fix from the last change did not actually take effect. The executor now tracks a per job and application key in a dictionary to avoid duplicate runs of the same job for the same application, but that only stops duplicate starts — it does not make a different application's run of the same job execute at the same time as the first. When the same job is triggered for all applications together, the console view shows only one application's job actually running while the other two immediately show load failed, instead of starting and running alongside the first.

Please look past the run-guard dictionary and find what is actually serializing execution. Check whether jobs are dispatched through a single worker, a thread or process pool with a concurrency limit of one, or any queue that processes one job at a time regardless of key. Also check whether the job itself depends on a shared resource that is not safe for concurrent use across applications, such as a single cluster login or token or session object, a shared working directory, temp file, or log path, or any client or context that gets overwritten when a second application's job starts while the first is still running. Any of these would explain why the second and third attempts fail immediately instead of running in parallel.

Fix this at the core job-execution layer so jobs for different applications can genuinely run at the same time, with any shared login, session, or client resource looked up and used per application rather than globally. Make this a general fix in the executor itself, not something specific to this one status job, so any job that needs to run concurrently across multiple applications benefits going forward. Also update the running-job-status popover so it lists every currently active run, one per application, instead of showing only one job at a time regardless of how many are actually running.

Separately, for the application whose load-failed row shows no last-loaded timestamp at all: check whether that application has genuinely never completed a successful run yet, in which case show a clear message such as no successful load yet instead of leaving it blank, or whether there is a bug in that application's cache lookup specifically, since the other two applications' failed rows do show a timestamp correctly.

Once changed, verify by triggering the job across all applications at the same time and confirming all of them show as actively running together in the console view and in the running-job-status popover, not just one at a time.
